[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-163599"] :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{3}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0={0}^{3}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}+t.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)={1}^{3}+1=2\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)={2}^{3}+2=10\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =150-6 \\\\  & =144\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-163659/selection/9d8fc435fd93abb179f4bb159e58b05c09943fe5f0b785b1e80c0fb828a37b07" :answer/id #uuid "4284d4c2-1466-52c7-9cc6-11f30e131536" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "226\\,J" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163659/selection/2f12fae682c7f18f26704d27be60b732c6afb1202a51956dd3f9176e97076399" :answer/id #uuid "df69aac9-c7c5-54d5-824a-bf994c63aba9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "246\\,J" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163659/selection/3a9a7d5c21589467f4ebd8b171b00d7cd5f13cbce3c6a16c3f0c540feff72a1f" :answer/id #uuid "2336017e-af89-5268-80d1-3366e68f6c33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "266\\,J" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163659/selection/aed6d016409aeb2ccd08fb5f8ccd7298097a78a1174e1c7e40805cdaf1bba20a" :answer/id #uuid "f3f6e6d2-e38b-5bfc-930e-be3cffb1e523" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "256\\,J" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163659/selection/768c7642a5f40cdf8fbc965a2defa266146e761d1840c29b21ecc1244fc16fe6" :answer/id #uuid "5daf8589-30ff-57e7-972a-ac9a860b0ca3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "236\\,J" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163659/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7b152d2-f454-5863-ab30-b02fb89044a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163659/selection/3a9a7d5c21589467f4ebd8b171b00d7cd5f13cbce3c6a16c3f0c540feff72a1f" :answer-field/choices ["q-163659/selection/9d8fc435fd93abb179f4bb159e58b05c09943fe5f0b785b1e80c0fb828a37b07" "q-163659/selection/2f12fae682c7f18f26704d27be60b732c6afb1202a51956dd3f9176e97076399" "q-163659/selection/3a9a7d5c21589467f4ebd8b171b00d7cd5f13cbce3c6a16c3f0c540feff72a1f" "q-163659/selection/aed6d016409aeb2ccd08fb5f8ccd7298097a78a1174e1c7e40805cdaf1bba20a" "q-163659/selection/768c7642a5f40cdf8fbc965a2defa266146e761d1840c29b21ecc1244fc16fe6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163659" :question/id #uuid "e3727b71-f77c-52d3-b736-d8fbf4a3880a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163659" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(4t+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=4t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n3=2(0)^{2}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{2}+t+3.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)^{2}+1+3=6\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)^{2}+2+3=13\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {13}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =338-72 \\\\  & =266\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-163659/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163659"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163673/selection/6b3480b7a615000132d4377a97ad898f8496c92915fa3e65b2778b113784f22c" :answer/id #uuid "96767dfb-287f-53bb-9196-e9d620e98a76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "200\\,J" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163673/selection/5500861f6f5cf0aeadfef1c2a08390e275e248a5d232dce00f3fc0ab53e43961" :answer/id #uuid "ab5d3f92-ebea-584a-8e8b-e995603ca420" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "150\\,J" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163673/selection/69a69515254a14b27926407e1cbfc15e123160ad279b060f691fafe76aa30945" :answer/id #uuid "5d5f901d-37ac-5a1b-8b4b-bbf11e937b61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "245\\,J" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163673/selection/5a2a79a29fcaa701302d7ef68e007c1f9501dd4346d8a72bc53c162567b3dbd1" :answer/id #uuid "b6805279-2beb-57c4-9a44-4dba5a9e7bc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "225\\,J" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163673/selection/89011a5ecf6762fd7359183e271567a49b054a76d164267497c98e590a7bfae4" :answer/id #uuid "7b85815e-ed10-5368-b547-e190b1eb3114" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "175\\,J" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163673/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "84bdd8e3-48b4-5c45-8734-a5b03f856bd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163673/selection/5a2a79a29fcaa701302d7ef68e007c1f9501dd4346d8a72bc53c162567b3dbd1" :answer-field/choices ["q-163673/selection/6b3480b7a615000132d4377a97ad898f8496c92915fa3e65b2778b113784f22c" "q-163673/selection/5500861f6f5cf0aeadfef1c2a08390e275e248a5d232dce00f3fc0ab53e43961" "q-163673/selection/69a69515254a14b27926407e1cbfc15e123160ad279b060f691fafe76aa30945" "q-163673/selection/5a2a79a29fcaa701302d7ef68e007c1f9501dd4346d8a72bc53c162567b3dbd1" "q-163673/selection/89011a5ecf6762fd7359183e271567a49b054a76d164267497c98e590a7bfae4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163673" :question/id #uuid "e4592e02-6784-5c14-ac84-e06efe25989a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163673" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $5t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=5t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=5(2)=10\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=5(4)=20\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (20)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (10)^{2} \\\\  & =300-75 \\\\  & =225\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-163673/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163673"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163692/selection/a1f1532c02551946dec059ab2a04d00163558b4ca60d3093e4dd518d036075b4" :answer/id #uuid "7faded0d-dd94-5361-9599-dca0e97eba37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163692/selection/3ee8486a0630768bbd20061576a72f8b9f3213b8c29b30f493fedf927485fb25" :answer/id #uuid "190ec5a7-8470-5e0b-b466-5dec532fa401" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163692/selection/11e878938ccef6cf5c36e1a9aabe7e08a32eec188fdcb429e4b5771c5336214e" :answer/id #uuid "a50b68df-0019-5469-846f-b2424c0dcbe9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163692/selection/21e85611efe70c486805e069c9b9d0b7f88a3c1616681df42691a3333e7c5043" :answer/id #uuid "9b9f6f27-1240-5e7f-9044-52f1a02f2ac8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163692/selection/b5bae007df3e90caa6ef56b76d451c4e6e63d1d0ee315bd80f4d2edca7f9a6de" :answer/id #uuid "e7d1654d-5a4f-5961-b207-5cd2d79460bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163692/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f453a824-002a-5487-8583-ac98dfe0cb53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163692/selection/21e85611efe70c486805e069c9b9d0b7f88a3c1616681df42691a3333e7c5043" :answer-field/choices ["q-163692/selection/a1f1532c02551946dec059ab2a04d00163558b4ca60d3093e4dd518d036075b4" "q-163692/selection/3ee8486a0630768bbd20061576a72f8b9f3213b8c29b30f493fedf927485fb25" "q-163692/selection/11e878938ccef6cf5c36e1a9aabe7e08a32eec188fdcb429e4b5771c5336214e" "q-163692/selection/21e85611efe70c486805e069c9b9d0b7f88a3c1616681df42691a3333e7c5043" "q-163692/selection/b5bae007df3e90caa6ef56b76d451c4e6e63d1d0ee315bd80f4d2edca7f9a6de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163692" :question/id #uuid "46c3ee13-bd0d-5a31-9035-b0ebb0177f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-163692" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $6\\sqrt{x}\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}6\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =4(8-1) \\\\  & =28\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 28 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ 28 & =\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}^{2} & =32 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =64 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $8\\,m/s.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-163692/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163692"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163701/selection/4ee0e99c951b9bc833b7700978c9cb20ce90e3b139ef0a76b8bbdf8f379e6af9" :answer/id #uuid "b16d7080-4375-51a9-9841-bd25ae33c760" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163701/selection/11e878938ccef6cf5c36e1a9aabe7e08a32eec188fdcb429e4b5771c5336214e" :answer/id #uuid "73df6564-c83f-567b-b8fc-8ce700d1c770" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163701/selection/d0a2bc45f37d5470927346bf68194b62a5f123847aba6762d3602d7b2a243460" :answer/id #uuid "111243b3-75ad-59a8-8c6f-281862ce9fae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163701/selection/31f0acf54d30170a6467d35ff6ae119531f7d099f66180fb1014f1b4a89d2afa" :answer/id #uuid "c82f8f22-25d7-52ca-b76b-dabc1e19edb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163701/selection/5200e937f430e7904fda7e01112e956259857a77ef8e7c35ff0b64bded683c47" :answer/id #uuid "e2f074fc-a84a-541c-bd56-cb94ece7dc31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163701/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd57831-65d5-5fcf-b175-4e962ee256ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163701/selection/31f0acf54d30170a6467d35ff6ae119531f7d099f66180fb1014f1b4a89d2afa" :answer-field/choices ["q-163701/selection/4ee0e99c951b9bc833b7700978c9cb20ce90e3b139ef0a76b8bbdf8f379e6af9" "q-163701/selection/11e878938ccef6cf5c36e1a9aabe7e08a32eec188fdcb429e4b5771c5336214e" "q-163701/selection/d0a2bc45f37d5470927346bf68194b62a5f123847aba6762d3602d7b2a243460" "q-163701/selection/31f0acf54d30170a6467d35ff6ae119531f7d099f66180fb1014f1b4a89d2afa" "q-163701/selection/5200e937f430e7904fda7e01112e956259857a77ef8e7c35ff0b64bded683c47"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163701" :question/id #uuid "4f55f932-ebf2-5609-b332-0f80ccbf81ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-163701" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $3x\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =24-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{2}\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{45}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{45}{2} & =\\frac{125}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=5-4=1\\,m/s.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-163701/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163701"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
